Task 1: Create a Persistent Volume (PV) and Persistent Volume Claim (PVC) for VM Storage
Subtask 1.1: Examine Available Storage Classes
First, let's explore the storage options available in your cluster.

List available storage classes:
oc get storageclass
Get detailed information about a specific storage class:
oc describe storageclass <storage-class-name>
Check for default storage class:
oc get storageclass -o jsonpath='{.items[?(@.metadata.annotations.storageclass\.kubernetes\.io/is-default-class=="true")].metadata.name}'
Subtask 1.2: Create a Persistent Volume Claim
Create a PVC that will be used for VM disk storage.

Create a PVC manifest file:
cat > vm-disk-pvc.yaml << 'EOF'
apiVersion: v1
kind: PersistentVolumeClaim
metadata:
  name: vm-disk-pvc
  namespace: default
  labels:
    app: vm-storage
spec:
  accessModes:
    - ReadWriteOnce
  resources:
    requests:
      storage: 10Gi
  storageClassName: "" # Use default storage class
EOF
Apply the PVC configuration:
oc apply -f vm-disk-pvc.yaml
Verify PVC creation and status:
oc get pvc vm-disk-pvc
Check PVC details:
oc describe pvc vm-disk-pvc
Subtask 1.3: Create an Additional Data Disk PVC
Create a second PVC for additional data storage.

Create a data disk PVC manifest:
cat > vm-data-pvc.yaml << 'EOF'
apiVersion: v1
kind: PersistentVolumeClaim
metadata:
  name: vm-data-pvc
  namespace: default
  labels:
    app: vm-data-storage
spec:
  accessModes:
    - ReadWriteOnce
  resources:
    requests:
      storage: 5Gi
  storageClassName: "" # Use default storage class
EOF
Apply the data PVC configuration:
oc apply -f vm-data-pvc.yaml
Verify both PVCs are available:
oc get pvc
Task 2: Attach the PVC to a Running VM
Subtask 2.1: Create a Virtual Machine with Persistent Storage
Create a VM that uses the persistent storage we configured.

Create a VM manifest with attached PVCs:
cat > vm-with-storage.yaml << 'EOF'
apiVersion: kubevirt.io/v1
kind: VirtualMachine
metadata:
  name: vm-with-persistent-storage
  namespace: default
  labels:
    app: storage-demo
spec:
  running: true
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        kubevirt.io/vm: vm-with-persistent-storage
    spec:
      domain:
        cpu:
          cores: 2
        memory:
          guest: 2Gi
        devices:
          disks:
          - name: rootdisk
            disk:
              bus: virtio
          - name: datadisk
            disk:
              bus: virtio
          - name: cloudinitdisk
            disk:
              bus: virtio
          interfaces:
          - name: default
            masquerade: {}
        resources:
          requests:
            memory: 2Gi
            cpu: 500m
      networks:
      - name: default
        pod: {}
      volumes:
      - name: rootdisk
        persistentVolumeClaim:
          claimName: vm-disk-pvc
      - name: datadisk
        persistentVolumeClaim:
          claimName: vm-data-pvc
      - name: cloudinitdisk
        cloudInitNoCloud:
          userData: |
            #cloud-config
            user: fedora
            password: fedora
            chpasswd: { expire: False }
            ssh_authorized_keys:
              - ssh-rsa AAAAB3NzaC1yc2EAAAADAQABAAABAQC... # Add your SSH key here
EOF
Apply the VM configuration:
oc apply -f vm-with-storage.yaml
Monitor VM creation:
oc get vm vm-with-persistent-storage
Check VM instance status:
oc get vmi vm-with-persistent-storage
Subtask 2.2: Verify VM is Running
Check VM status:
oc get vm vm-with-persistent-storage -o jsonpath='{.status.printableStatus}'
Get VM details:
oc describe vm vm-with-persistent-storage
Check associated pods:
oc get pods -l kubevirt.io/vm=vm-with-persistent-storage
Subtask 2.3: Access the Virtual Machine
Connect to VM console:
virtctl console vm-with-persistent-storage
Note: If virtctl is not available, you can use the OpenShift web console to access the VM console.

Alternative: Use SSH (if configured):
# First, get the VM's IP address
oc get vmi vm-with-persistent-storage -o jsonpath='{.status.interfaces[0].ipAddress}'

# Then SSH to the VM
ssh fedora@<VM_IP_ADDRESS>
Task 3: Verify Disk Functionality Within the VM
Subtask 3.1: Examine Disk Configuration
Once connected to the VM, verify the attached disks.

List available block devices:
lsblk
Check disk information:
fdisk -l
View disk usage:
df -h
Subtask 3.2: Prepare and Mount the Data Disk
Format and mount the additional data disk.

Create a partition on the data disk (assuming it's /dev/vdb):
sudo fdisk /dev/vdb
Follow these steps in fdisk:

Press n for new partition
Press p for primary partition
Press 1 for partition number
Press Enter twice to accept default start and end sectors
Press w to write changes
Format the partition:
sudo mkfs.ext4 /dev/vdb1
Create a mount point:
sudo mkdir /mnt/data
Mount the data disk:
sudo mount /dev/vdb1 /mnt/data
Verify the mount:
df -h /mnt/data
Subtask 3.3: Test Data Persistence
Test that data persists across VM restarts.

Create test data on the data disk:
sudo touch /mnt/data/test-file.txt
echo "This is persistent data" | sudo tee /mnt/data/test-file.txt
Create test data on the root disk:
echo "Root disk data" | sudo tee ~/root-test.txt
Make the mount permanent (add to /etc/fstab):
echo "/dev/vdb1 /mnt/data ext4 defaults 0 2" | sudo tee -a /etc/fstab
Exit the VM console:
exit
Subtask 3.4: Test VM Restart and Data Persistence
Stop the VM:
oc patch vm vm-with-persistent-storage --type merge -p '{"spec":{"running":false}}'
Wait for VM to stop:
oc get vm vm-with-persistent-storage -w
Start the VM again:
oc patch vm vm-with-persistent-storage --type merge -p '{"spec":{"running":true}}'
Wait for VM to be ready:
oc get vmi vm-with-persistent-storage -w
Reconnect to the VM:
virtctl console vm-with-persistent-storage
Verify data persistence:
# Check if data disk is mounted
df -h /mnt/data

# Verify test files exist
cat /mnt/data/test-file.txt
cat ~/root-test.txt
Advanced Configuration and Best Practices
Storage Performance Optimization
Check storage performance:
# Inside the VM
sudo dd if=/dev/zero of=/mnt/data/testfile bs=1M count=100 oflag=direct
Monitor I/O performance:
iostat -x 1 5
Storage Expansion
Expand PVC size:
oc patch pvc vm-data-pvc -p '{"spec":{"resources":{"requests":{"storage":"8Gi"}}}}'
Verify expansion:
oc get pvc vm-data-pvc
Backup and Snapshot Considerations
Create a VM snapshot (if supported by storage class):
cat > vm-snapshot.yaml << 'EOF'
apiVersion: snapshot.kubevirt.io/v1alpha1
kind: VirtualMachineSnapshot
metadata:
  name: vm-snapshot-1
  namespace: default
spec:
  source:
    apiGroup: kubevirt.io
    kind: VirtualMachine
    name: vm-with-persistent-storage
EOF
Apply snapshot configuration:
oc apply -f vm-snapshot.yaml
Troubleshooting Common Issues
Issue 1: PVC Stuck in Pending State
Symptoms: PVC remains in "Pending" status

Solutions:

Check available storage classes:
oc get storageclass
Verify storage class supports dynamic provisioning:
oc describe storageclass <storage-class-name>
Check for sufficient cluster resources:
oc get nodes
oc describe nodes
Issue 2: VM Cannot Start Due to Storage Issues
Symptoms: VM fails to start with storage-related errors

Solutions:

Check PVC status:
oc get pvc
oc describe pvc <pvc-name>
Verify VM configuration:
oc describe vm vm-with-persistent-storage
Check events for error messages:
oc get events --sort-by=.metadata.creationTimestamp
Issue 3: Disk Not Visible in VM
Symptoms: Attached disk doesn't appear in the VM

Solutions:

Verify disk attachment in VM spec:
oc get vm vm-with-persistent-storage -o yaml | grep -A 10 volumes
Check VM events:
oc describe vmi vm-with-persistent-storage
Restart the VM:
oc patch vm vm-with-persistent-storage --type merge -p '{"spec":{"running":false}}'
oc patch vm vm-with-persistent-storage --type merge -p '{"spec":{"running":true}}'
Cleanup
To clean up the resources created in this lab:

Stop and delete the VM:
oc delete vm vm-with-persistent-storage
Delete the PVCs:
oc delete pvc vm-disk-pvc vm-data-pvc
Remove manifest files:
rm -f vm-disk-pvc.yaml vm-data-pvc.yaml vm-with-storage.yaml vm-snapshot.yaml
